# family: transport
# bridge: A(permutation-golden)
# regime: bit-exact
# instrument-first: accd36e260a5
# baseline: tests/unit/transport
# baseline-count: 56
#
# The unit is LocalMultiplayerPeer and LocalLoopbackSession, which are one
# bidirectional friend pair and cross together. The instrument is
# tests/native/goldens/transport_permutations.txt, recorded from the GDScript
# arm by tests/crossing/transport_arm.gd before any of it was native, and the
# regime is bit-exact because the link simulator is integers and a permutation
# all the way down.
#
# Three decisions were taken at unit-open, before any C++ was written.
#
# The permutation is CONTRACT, not incidental. Both tiers draw from the
# engine's own RandomNumberGenerator, hash the stream name with the engine's
# own String hash, and break ties with the engine's own stable sort, so
# reproducing the GDScript arm's exact delivery order costs nothing beyond
# spelling the derivation the same way. Asserting only the property, that every
# packet arrives once and out of order, would throw away the one instrument
# that can catch a stream drawn one time too many.
#
# Peer IDs stay RANDOM. They are drawn from the global generator and they are
# the reason nothing in the corpus may hardcode a peer id, which is a real bug
# class this transport is meant to catch. LoopbackRig::peer_id(index) is how a
# case addresses a peer instead.
#
# LinkConditions SPLITS OUT as a registered LocalLinkConditions, on the codec
# family's precedent. Absorbing it into the session would turn five preset
# constructors into session methods and make every caller name a session to
# describe a link.
#
# LoopbackRig's arrival here also closes the native half the interface doc's T8
# left owed: its interest assertion needs a NetwMultiplayer built treeless from
# C++, which is this rig and nothing else.
#
# THE SESSION SUITES LEFT THIS SCOPE AT W3, and this ledger shrank with
# them. test_session_assignment_edge.gd and test_session_bringup.gd were
# never transport's specification: every one of their 38 rows read CARRY
# ... crosses with the session column, which is where they now live, at
# tests/unit/session. The rows moved to session.ledger and baseline-count
# fell 94 -> 56.
#
# It is a re-file rather than a deletion, and the gate cannot tell the two
# apart on its own: the deletion check reads the census's baseline commit
# through git ls-tree, so a census RETAKEN after the move is what makes the
# shrunken ledger legal. Retaking it is step 3 of the move, not a follow-up.
#
# The move was forced rather than chosen. crossing-gate balances every
# ledger claiming a scope against that scope's whole census count, so a
# scope belongs to exactly one crossing family, permanently. A second
# family porting a case out of tests/unit/transport would have made THIS
# ledger refuse, however honest its own rows were.
#
# Two things die with the arm they compared: transport_arm.gd, and the stand-in
# NetwHarnessLoopbackPeer in extension/tests/test_networked_harness.h.
